From: lmoresi <louis.moresi@anu.edu.au>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 06:33:53 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 3/8] The cut refuses a flat cell

cut_along_lines refused an inverted cell but not a flat one. With the
edge labelling now complete across in-cell kinks, add_fault placed
traces it used to refuse for other reasons, and some came back with a
cell of area 1e-17 to 6e-19 of their own size (a 0-degree angle), at
90- and 120-degree turns where several samples lost their vertex to a
later one (UNFILED 72). That holds both for the every-sample pull and
for a lighter one. A flat cell gives the finite-element map a zero
Jacobian.

The cut now raises when a cell's area is below 1e-9 of its own longest
edge squared (0.433 for an equilateral cell). Over every mesh add_fault
returned in the measurement (about 1060, three pull rules), the flat
ones sit below 1e-14 and the sound ones at 5.7e-5 and above
(development's worst; nearly all at 1e-2 and above), with nothing
between. The threshold is the log-middle of that gap. Being relative to
each cell's size, it reads alike on a graded mesh. It is a flatness
guard, not a quality guard: development returns a poor but not flat
cell at 5.7e-5, and it passes.

The message names the worst cell, the trace's nearest sample, and the
sharpest turn within three cells, and says how to fix it: a vertex at a
sharp turn, a smaller angle, or samples about a cell apart. The count is
reduced by SUM and the worst cell by a MIN over (ratio, size, position),
so every rank raises the same message.

test_0899: the measured trace is refused when its samples are pulled
sparsely and when every sample is pulled; a trace that places normally
is not. With the guard disabled the two refusals fail. The fault,
conforming-surface and line-cut suites: 431 passed, 1 skipped.

